# Eclipses — a selection of real eclipses from 1980 to 2099 to visit, solar
# and lunar, in time order.
#
# A solar eclipse (the moon in front of the sun) has a moment (universal
# time) and a place where it is central (total or annular) or, for the one
# partial eclipse, its deepest point. The dates and kinds are the published
# ones (NASA's Five Millennium Canon); the moments and places were refined
# with lib/Moon itself, along each eclipse's central path near the
# published greatest eclipse, so the sky shows each one at its deepest.
# They differ from the published greatest eclipse by minutes and up to a
# few hundred kilometers along the path.
#
# A lunar eclipse (the moon in the earth's shadow, `lunar`) is seen from
# the whole night side of the earth at once. Its moment is its greatest
# eclipse as lib/Moon's lunarShadow finds it, within minutes of the
# published one, and its place one where the moon stands about 28 degrees
# up then: 62 degrees north of the point under the moon (at most 66 north).
#
# Case 1258 checks that lib/Moon puts the moon over the sun at each solar
# one, and in the earth's umbra at each lunar one.
#
# The settings jump to one with eclipseClock: it turns the universal time
# into the local solar time the clock runs on (the longitude's hours), so
# the sky shows the eclipse from that place.
import Math
open Calendar

enum EclipseKind { total, annular, hybrid, partial }

type Eclipse {
  year: Int
  month: Int
  day: Int
  # the moment of greatest eclipse, hours of universal time
  utHours: Float
  kind: EclipseKind
  # the moon in the earth's shadow (a lunar eclipse), or the sun behind
  # the moon (a solar one)
  lunar: Bool = false
  # where to see it (degrees north, east): a solar one's deepest point, a
  # lunar one's place with the moon well up
  latitude: Float
  longitude: Float
}

